.TH BOSWAS-TASK-MANAGER 1 "November 2026" "boswas-apps 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-task-manager \- see what is running on the computer, how busy it is, and end tasks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-task-manager
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR page ]
.br
.B boswas-task-manager
.B \-\-version
|
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-task-manager
is the Task Manager of Boswas OS. The desktop starts it with Ctrl+Shift+Esc.
It reads the kernel's own figures under
.I /proc
and
.I /sys
and talks to systemd and logind over D-Bus; it starts no other program to
measure anything. It runs as the user who started it and never as root.
.PP
Only one Task Manager runs in a session. Starting the program again brings
the window to the front and, with
.BR \-\-page ,
shows that page.
It has one window, as Task Manager has on Windows: a second one would
measure the computer twice and show the same. A start with
.B \-\-new\-window
brings the window forward as well.
.SS Pages
.TP
.B processes
What is running, as rows: an application with everything it started is one
row that can be opened; then the other programs of the user (background
processes) and those of the system, the kernel threads among them. A program
counts as an application when it runs as the user in a user session and its
executable, its script (when the executable is an interpreter) or its name
matches the program or the StartupWMClass of a visible desktop entry that
does not run in a terminal. Columns: status, CPU (100% is all processors
busy), memory (resident memory not shared with other processes), disk
(bytes read from and written to storage per second, where the kernel lets
the user see it). The header shows the totals; cells are tinted by how much
they use. Network use per process is not shown: the kernel does not tell it
to an ordinary user.
.TP
.B performance
Graphs of the last 60 samples: CPU (optionally one graph per logical
processor), memory with its composition, every disk, every network
interface, graphics adapters (a graph only when the driver reports
utilisation), and the battery.
.TP
.B startup
The programs that start when the user signs in: the XDG autostart entries
that apply to this desktop. Disabling or enabling one writes the user's own
file of the same name with Hidden=true or Hidden=false; the system's file
is not touched. An installed application can be added, and the user's own
entries removed.
.TP
.B users
The sessions logind knows, with the processes of each. Without logind, the
user's own processes as one session.
.TP
.B details
Every process: PID, status, user, CPU, resident and proportional memory,
threads, nice value, start time, command line. From here a process can be
ended, with everything below it if wanted, sent a signal, given another
priority, and its properties shown (never its environment).
.TP
.B services
The services of the system's and of the user's systemd manager. Start, Stop
and Restart ask systemd, which asks for authorisation where it needs it.
.TP
.B settings
Update speed (0.5, 1 or 4 seconds, or paused), the page to start with, a
command line column on the Processes page, and the theme.
.SS Ending a task
End task asks the process to end (SIGTERM). If it is still there after
three seconds, Task Manager offers to force it (SIGKILL). A signal is only
sent when the process ID still belongs to the process that was selected.
.SS Keys
Ctrl+N or Alt+N: run a new task (a command line that is split like a shell
does but started without one). Delete: end the selected task. F5: read
everything again now. Ctrl+F: the filter box. Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab:
the next and the previous page. Alt+Enter: properties. Ctrl+C: copy the
selected row. Typing in a list goes to the row that starts with what was
typed.
.SS Units
Sizes are binary: 1 KB is 1024 bytes. Network rates are decimal bits per
second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " page"
Open with this page: processes, performance, startup, users, details,
services or settings.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short help text and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (also when a running Task Manager was told to come forward),
2 for a page that does not exist, 4 when started as root.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.boswas.TaskManager/settings.json
The settings.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/com.boswas.TaskManager/window.ini
The size of the window.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/autostart/
Where the user's startup entries and overrides are written.
.TP
.IR /etc/xdg/autostart/ ", " $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS/autostart/
The startup entries installed for everybody (read only).
.TP
.IR /usr/share/misc/pci.ids
When installed, gives graphics adapters their model name.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas-files (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR loginctl (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR renice (1)
